an for not feeling well' (score=0.74):]\n  Source: Clinical Review: The 2011 report on dietary reference intake for vitamin D: where do we go from here? > Adverse Effects\n  According to the CDC, hypercalcemia (serum levels >10.5 mg/dL [2.63 mmol/L]) and hypercalciuria (>250 mg/d in women, >275 mg/d in men) are rare in healthy individuals, typically associated with condit\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.80):]\n  Source: Biochemistry_Lippinco\n  Choose the ONE best answer. .1. A 30-year-old woman of Northern European ancestry presents with progressive dyspnea (shortness of breath). She denies the use of cigarettes. Family history reveals that\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.88):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Paraneoplastic Syndromes: Endocrinologic/Hematologic 610 Another relatively common cause of HHM is excess production of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Like granulomatous disorders associated with hypercalce\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  abdominal pain and trouble focusing → hypercalcemia → Endocrine / Metabolic Disorders (Including Vitamin D Toxicity)\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess serum calcium and vitamin D levels to evaluate for hypercalcemia, which could indicate antacid-induced toxicity or malignancy-related calcium dysregulation\n  abdominal pain, weight loss, and trouble focusing → hypercalcemia → Breast Cancer with Bone Metastases\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Investigate hypercalcemia as a potential link to malignancy, particularly breast cancer with bone metastases, given the systemic symptoms and weight loss\n  abdominal pain and weight loss → gastrointestinal infiltration by plasma cells → Multiple Myeloma\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Consider GI tract involvement in multiple myeloma (e.g., via biopsy or imaging) to explain abdominal symptoms alongside systemic features\n\nWhen indirect_